    "officialTopicCodes": false,
    "designNotes": "The CED has no numbered topics and no named units: each of nine units (~15 class periods each) is defined by a list of reading and writing skills (codes 1.A-8.C, grouped in 8 skill categories). Topic codes here are ALTON-assigned 'U<unit>.<n>', each grouping one or two official skills from that unit's skill list. Unit titles and topic titles are ALTON labels summarizing each unit's overview and skill focus (in the CED, units 8 and 9 mainly revisit skills). Skill codes in `skills` are official. Unit skill lists are transcribed from the Course at a Glance; topic skills sometimes add skills from the same unit. Topic U9.3 (the three FRQ types) is an ALTON addition to schedule timed-essay practice; it is not an official unit topic. Sub-keywords, ordering, prerequisites and estLessons are ALTON editorial designs. Exam: 45 MC (23-25 reading, 20-22 writing; 60 min, 45%), 3 FRQ (synthesis, rhetorical analysis, argument; 2 h 15 min including a 15-min reading period, 55%). Only MC skill-category weights are published; no per-unit weights."
  },
  "skills": [
    {
      "code": "1.A",
      "category": "Rhetorical Situation - Reading",
      "label": "Identify and describe components of the rhetorical situation: the exigence, audience, writer, purpose, context, and message."
    },
    {
      "code": "1.B",
      "category": "Rhetorical Situation - Reading",
      "label": "Explain how an argument demonstrates understanding of an audience's beliefs, values, or needs."
    },
    {
      "code": "2.A",
      "category": "Rhetorical Situation - Writing",
      "label": "Write introductions and conclusions appropriate to the purpose and context of the rhetorical situation."
    },
    {
      "code": "2.B",
      "category": "Rhetorical Situation - Writing",
      "label": "Demonstrate an understanding of an audience's beliefs, values, or needs."
    },
    {
      "code": "3.A",
      "category": "Claims and Evidence - Reading",
      "label": "Identify and explain claims and evidence within an argument."
    },
    {
      "code": "3.B",
      "category": "Claims and Evidence - Reading",
      "label": "Identify and describe the overarching thesis of an argument, and any indication it provides of the argument's structure."
    },
    {
      "code": "3.C",
      "category": "Claims and Evidence - Reading",
      "label": "Explain ways claims are qualified through modifiers, counterarguments, and alternative perspectives."
    },
    {
      "code": "4.A",
      "category": "Claims and Evidence - Writing",
      "label": "Develop a paragraph that includes a claim and evidence supporting the claim."
    },
    {
      "code": "4.B",
      "category": "Claims and Evidence - Writing",
      "label": "Write a thesis statement that requires proof or defense and that may preview the structure of the argument."
    },
    {
      "code": "4.C",
      "category": "Claims and Evidence - Writing",
      "label": "Qualify a claim using modifiers, counterarguments, or alternative perspectives."
    },
    {
      "code": "5.A",
      "category": "Reasoning and Organization - Reading",
      "label": "Describe the line of reasoning and explain whether it supports an argument's overarching thesis."
    },
    {
      "code": "5.B",
      "category": "Reasoning and Organization - Reading",
      "label": "Explain how the organization of a text creates unity and coherence and reflects a line of reasoning."
    },
    {
      "code": "5.C",
      "category": "Reasoning and Organization - Reading",
      "label": "Recognize and explain the use of methods of development to accomplish a purpose."
    },
    {
      "code": "6.A",
      "category": "Reasoning and Organization - Writing",
      "label": "Develop a line of reasoning and commentary that explains it throughout an argument."
    },
    {
      "code": "6.B",
      "category": "Reasoning and Organization - Writing",
      "label": "Use transitional elements to guide the reader through the line of reasoning of an argument."
    },
    {
      "code": "6.C",
      "category": "Reasoning and Organization - Writing",
      "label": "Use appropriate methods of development to advance an argument."
    },
    {
      "code": "7.A",
      "category": "Style - Reading",
      "label": "Explain how word choice, comparisons, and syntax contribute to the specific tone or style of a text."
    },
    {
      "code": "7.B",
      "category": "Style - Reading",
      "label": "Explain how writers create, combine, and place independent and dependent clauses to show relationships between and among ideas."
    },
    {
      "code": "7.C",
      "category": "Style - Reading",
      "label": "Explain how grammar and mechanics contribute to the clarity and effectiveness of an argument."
    },
    {
      "code": "8.A",
      "category": "Style - Writing",
      "label": "Strategically use words, comparisons, and syntax to convey a specific tone or style in an argument."
    },
    {
      "code": "8.B",
      "category": "Style - Writing",
      "label": "Write sentences that clearly convey ideas and arguments."
    },
    {
      "code": "8.C",
      "category": "Style - Writing",
      "label": "Use established conventions of grammar and mechanics to communicate clearly and effectively."
    }
  ],
